# 2. The logic lives in a library crate

Date: {{ date }}

## Status

Accepted

## Context

This repository is a workspace: `crates/{{ name }}-core` holds the logic and
`crates/{{ name }}` is the binary. A single crate with the same code in
`src/lib.rs` and a `[[bin]]` alongside it would compile just as well, so the
split has to earn itself.

## Decision

Two crates, published separately, sharing one version from
`[workspace.package]`.

The library is a dependency anyone can take without taking the command-line
surface with it: its dependency list stays small, its errors are an enum a
caller can match on rather than an `anyhow` chain meant for a human, and its
reference is rustdoc on docs.rs rather than this repository's README. The
binary keeps `clap`, `dialoguer` and the printing, and none of that reaches a
program that only wanted the logic.

One version for both, because they are released together. A separately
versioned library in the same repository means two changelogs, two tags, and a
question - "which library does this binary use?" - that has to be answered by
reading a lockfile.

Publishing is ordered: the library first, then a wait for the index, then the
binary. crates.io resolves the dependency at publish time and refuses a package
whose dependency it cannot see yet.

## Consequences

Both crates need a trusted publisher on crates.io, and the first version of
each has to be published by hand - a trusted publisher cannot be attached to a
crate that does not exist. Forgetting the second one fails with a message about
an invalid token, which is not what is wrong.

Moving a function from the binary into the library is a breaking change for
nobody and a gift to the next caller; moving one the other way is a promise
withdrawn. Prefer putting new logic in the library.
